                  "MEDIUM"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-49854",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Tornado has out-of-bounds memory access via C extension"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "### Summary\n\nTornado's optional native extension `tornado.speedups` implements `websocket_mask` without validating that the `mask` argument is exactly four bytes long. The C function reads four bytes from `mask` unconditionally, even when Python passes a shorter byte string. This can read beyond the provided buffer, exposing up to 3 bytes of uninitialized memory.\n\nThe behavior is reachable from Tornado's XSRF token decoder when `xsrf_cookies=True` and the native extension is active. \n\n### Mitigations\n\nThis bug is fixed in Tornado 6.5.6. Prior to upgrading to this version, setting the environment variable TORNADO_EXTENSION=0 will disable the vulnerable code (at the expense of reducing websocket performance)."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-49854",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-49854\nSeverity: LOW\nPackage: tornado\nFixed Version: 6.5.6\nLink: [CVE-2026-49854](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-49854)\n### Summary\n\nTornado's optional native extension `tornado.speedups` implements `websocket_mask` without validating that the `mask` argument is exactly four bytes long. The C function reads four bytes from `mask` unconditionally, even when Python passes a shorter byte string. This can read beyond the provided buffer, exposing up to 3 bytes of uninitialized memory.\n\nThe behavior is reachable from Tornado's XSRF token decoder when `xsrf_cookies=True` and the native extension is active. \n\n### Mitigations\n\nThis bug is fixed in Tornado 6.5.6. Prior to upgrading to this version, setting the environment variable TORNADO_EXTENSION=0 will disable the vulnerable code (at the expense of reducing websocket performance).",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-49854**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|LOW|tornado|6.5.6|[CVE-2026-49854](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-49854)|\n\n### Summary\n\nTornado's optional native extension `tornado.speedups` implements `websocket_mask` without validating that the `mask` argument is exactly four bytes long. The C function reads four bytes from `mask` unconditionally, even when Python passes a shorter byte string. This can read beyond the provided buffer, exposing up to 3 bytes of uninitialized memory.\n\nThe behavior is reachable from Tornado's XSRF token decoder when `xsrf_cookies=True` and the native extension is active. \n\n### Mitigations\n\nThis bug is fixed in Tornado 6.5.6. Prior to upgrading to this version, setting the environment variable TORNADO_EXTENSION=0 will disable the vulnerable code (at the expense of reducing websocket performance)."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 3.7,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:H/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:N",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "3.7",
                "tags": [
                  "vulnerability",
                  "security",
                  "LOW"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-24049",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "wheel: wheel: Privilege Escalation or Arbitrary Code Execution via malicious wheel file unpacking"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "wheel is a command line tool for manipulating Python wheel files, as defined in PEP 427. In versions 0.40.0 through 0.46.1, the unpack function is vulnerable to file permission modification through mishandling of file permissions after extraction. The logic blindly trusts the filename from the archive header for the chmod operation, even though the extraction process itself might have sanitized the path. Attackers can craft a malicious wheel file that, when unpacked, changes the permissions of critical system files (e.g., /etc/passwd, SSH keys, config files), allowing for Privilege Escalation or arbitrary code execution by modifying now-writable scripts. This issue has been fixed in version 0.46.2."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-24049",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-24049\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: wheel\nFixed Version: 0.46.2\nLink: [CVE-2026-24049](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-24049)\nwheel is a command line tool for manipulating Python wheel files, as defined in PEP 427. In versions 0.40.0 through 0.46.1, the unpack function is vulnerable to file permission modification through mishandling of file permissions after extraction. The logic blindly trusts the filename from the archive header for the chmod operation, even though the extraction process itself might have sanitized the path. Attackers can craft a malicious wheel file that, when unpacked, changes the permissions of critical system files (e.g., /etc/passwd, SSH keys, config files), allowing for Privilege Escalation or arbitrary code execution by modifying now-writable scripts. This issue has been fixed in version 0.46.2.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-24049**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|wheel|0.46.2|[CVE-2026-24049](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-24049)|\n\nwheel is a command line tool for manipulating Python wheel files, as defined in PEP 427. In versions 0.40.0 through 0.46.1, the unpack function is vulnerable to file permission modification through mishandling of file permissions after extraction. The logic blindly trusts the filename from the archive header for the chmod operation, even though the extraction process itself might have sanitized the path. Attackers can craft a malicious wheel file that, when unpacked, changes the permissions of critical system files (e.g., /etc/passwd, SSH keys, config files), allowing for Privilege Escalation or arbitrary code execution by modifying now-writable scripts. This issue has been fixed in version 0.46.2."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 7.1,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:H",
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "7.1",
                "tags": [
